    "reasoning": "Each answer below uses only its own captured worked solution. No submitted grades were provided. Fifteen fields have mathematically correct choices matching their source answers. q-79665 has an incomplete general solution under the stated real domain, and q-42790 contains an intermediate transcription error despite its correct final answer.\n\nq-158014: Separating gives dy/y=cos(x) dx, hence ln|y|=sin(x)+C and y=K exp(sin(x)), with K nonzero.\nq-158012: Integrating 3y^2 dy=2x dx gives y^3=x^2+C. The cube-root solution applies on intervals where y is nonzero, since the original equation divides by y^2.\nq-158010: Integrating dy/y=dx/sqrt(1-x^2) gives y=K exp(arcsin(x)), with K nonzero and |x|<1.\nq-79665: The original real equation requires xy>0, allowing both x,y>0 and x,y<0. MA splits sqrt(xy) into sqrt(x)sqrt(y), thereby discarding the negative sector. Its squared formula also requires x^(3/2)/3+K>0 to satisfy y'=sqrt(xy). The full real solution is y=sgn(x)(|x|^(3/2)/3+K)^2 on connected intervals with x nonzero and |x|^(3/2)/3+K>0. Differentiation gives y'=(|x|^(3/2)/3+K)sqrt(|x|)=sqrt(xy). Option 4 represents only the positive sector with the necessary restriction; no supplied choice gives the full stated general solution. Therefore correct_option is null, and MA's answer is preserved separately.\nq-42790: The sine argument is -2pi/3 or -pi/3 modulo 2pi. Solving gives x=-7pi/4 or -3pi/4 modulo 6pi, so a=-7pi/4. The source incorrectly repeats -2pi/3 in both columns of its first rearrangement; the second column should contain -pi/3. Its final answer is correct.\nq-121066: tan(3x)=-1/sqrt(3), so 3x=-pi/6+npi and a=-1/18 modulo 1/3. The supplied correct choice is -1/18.\nq-78957: Integration by parts with u=ln(x), v=3x^3 gives 3x^3 ln(x)-x^3+C.\nq-114553: Changing base and integrating by parts gives x^2(2ln(x)-1)/(4ln(2))+C.\nq-114555: Taking u=ln(3x), v=2x^4 gives 2x^4 ln(3x)-x^4/2+C.\nq-114579: Since the derivative of ln(x^3) is 3/x on its real domain x>0, integration by parts gives x ln(x^3)-3x+C.\nq-50222: Taking u=x^2 and v=-cos(3x)/3 gives the selected reduction formula. Further integration yields -x^2 cos(3x)/3+2x sin(3x)/9+2cos(3x)/27+C, confirming the formula.\nq-114942: An antiderivative is x^3(3ln^2(x)-2ln(x)+2/3)/ln^2(2). Evaluation at e and 1 gives (5e^3-2)/(3ln^2(2)).\nq-114944: On [1,e], the integrand is 4ln^2(x)/ln^2(3). Its antiderivative is 4x(ln^2(x)-2ln(x)+2)/ln^2(3), giving (4e-8)/ln^2(3).\nq-77776: The transformation sends (0,-1) to (3,-2) and (1,3) to (-10,6). Their image line has slope -8/13 and equation 8x+13y+2=0.\nq-8309: Integration gives sin(2y)/2=-cos(3x)/3+C. The initial condition sets C=1/3, giving 3sin(2y)=2(1-cos(3x)). The stated y range selects y=arcsin(2(1-cos(3x))/3)/2, locally on the interval containing zero where the argument is less than 1.\nq-50220: For the nonzero solution, separation gives 1/y=x+C; y(1)=1 sets C=0, hence y=1/x on x>0. The source's intermediate claim of a general solution omits the equilibrium y=0, but this does not affect the requested particular solution.",
